WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
I know that the phrase "sentinel chickens"
does sound a bit ridiculous.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.120
These chickens here in Griffith,
in rural Australia,

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.520
are not keeping watch for anything.
They're not on guard.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.260
They are just... chickens.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.320
But they are helping keep folks
in this area of New South Wales

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.580
safe from some nasty diseases.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.700
Diseases like Murray Valley
Encephalitis, MVE.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.500
It infects animals,
including humans,

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.180
and it's carried by mosquitoes.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.440
It has no vaccine, no cure.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.720
Most people who get it will
suffer fever and nausea,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.940
but about one in a thousand
will get seriously ill,

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.400
with a chance of life-changing
brain injuries, or death.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.700
If that virus is starting to flare up
in the animal population around here,

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.580
mosquito transmission means
it can easily spread to humans,

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:38.220
so it'll be really useful
for the local health department

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.140
to have an early warning system.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.760
- We have eight flocks at the moment,
and there are 15 birds per flock.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.320
They are usually between 9 and 14
weeks old when we get them,

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:50.400
at the start of the season.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.160
They are fed, watered
and they lay eggs.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:57.060
And that's an additional bonus for the
person that's looking after our coop.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:57.960
They get to keep the eggs.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.840
- Ideally, the researchers would
track levels of the virus

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.000
in wild animals and birds.
That's where it thrives.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.420
But that plan involves catching
and testing wild animals.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:07.440
Not practical.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.300
So instead, chickens!

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.520
They don't get sick from MVE
or other diseases like it,

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:12.720
and they can't pass
it on either.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.940
But if they get infected,
their immune system

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:16.980
still fights it,
still creates antibodies.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.320
The technical term
is "seroconversion".

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:22.500
And these chickens are outside
every hour of every day,

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.960
and the mosquitoes will
bite them a lot.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.440
- We take a small amount of blood
from each chicken, weekly.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.380
They collect the chickens,
and then we do a bleeding process

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.860
where they take a small amount
of blood via a lancet

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.920
from under the wing, collected on
a small blotting strip.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.420
It's just a very small pinprick.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.820
The chickens have a leg band on,

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:45.540
so that we know very specifically
what bird and what sample,

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:46.440
and we can match them up.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.840
And that goes off to
our labs in Sydney

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:53.940
where they do a number of tests for
a whole range of mosquito-borne viruses.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.920
The mosquito levels are
important as well,

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.260
but it's not enough
just to track mosquitoes.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:00.840
It's important that we monitor
a reservoir

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.480
that can give us a
better indication, quicker.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:05.280
The chickens give us a
pretty early warning,

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.360
within a couple of weeks.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.880
If we saw seroconversions
in our flocks,

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.460
we would ramp up the local media
to get that message out:

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:15.120
use a repellent, not be outside
at dawn and dusk,

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:16.860
and wear light,
loose-fitting clothing.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.540
Camping groups,
fishing groups,

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.600
those people that are visiting areas
get the message as well.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.700
We'll provide information
to local GPs

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.460
so that they will be
looking for symptoms.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.860
And there are other flocks
in other countries, I'm sure,

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:30.480
as part of their
surveillance program.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.720
- Of course, once a chicken has
tested positive, its work is done.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.160
It will always have
those antibodies,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.360
it will always test positive.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:37.860
So at that point,
it is retired,

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.300
and I'm assured that's
not a euphemism,

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.340
and testing continues on
the rest of the flock.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.305
I was a bit worried about
animal safety and cruelty here, but...

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:45.840
Do you mind?!

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.820
...the chickens are well cared for!

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.800
They're just having a blood sample
taken occasionally.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.440
In a world with factory farming
and fried chicken shops,

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.960
this does seem fairly low down
on the scale of

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:56.760
"things humanity
does to chickens".

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:00.360
- We really have no other way
of knowing what virus

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:01.440
might be around in the area.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:02.820
And they do save lives.

00:03:03.838 --> 00:03:04.980
- Never work with animals(!)

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:09.180
Th- This is not food!
These are my fingers!

